build:
  toolchain: stable 1.99.1, aarch64 Linux container; nightly-2026-10-01 for Miri
  features:
  - swar-number-parsing
  - serde_impl
  - runtime-detection
  system_libraries: []
  lockfile: 'Tag has no Cargo.lock. Generated with `cargo +nightly-2026-10-01 generate-lockfile
    -Zunstable-options --publish-time 2025-09-04T08:11:09Z` (the tag commit''s time).
    Key resolutions: halfbrown 0.3.0 (the only 0.3.x ever published, so also what
    any fresh resolve gives), hashbrown 0.15.5, value-trait 0.11.0, simdutf8 0.1.5,
    serde 1.0.219, serde_json 1.0.143. Kept as projects/simd-json/base-Cargo.lock.dated
    and in each repro folder. Fix commits got their own lock with --publish-time at
    the fix commit''s date.'
  build_result: 'Plain `cargo build --locked` FAILS on stable 1.99.1: `#![deny(warnings)]`
    (src/lib.rs:1) turns 3 unused_imports warnings into errors (src/numberparse/correct.rs:12,
    src/lib.rs:47, src/stage2.rs:3). Builds with `RUSTFLAGS="--cap-lints warn" cargo
    build --locked`. Downstream users are unaffected since cargo caps lints on registry
    dependencies. Same warnings appear for the x86_64 target under nightly Miri, so
    this is not aarch64 specific.'
  test_result: '`RUSTFLAGS="--cap-lints warn" cargo test --locked --all-targets --no-fail-fast`:
    443 passed, 0 failed, 2 ignored (lib 313+2 ignored, jsonchecker 126, serenity
    2, weird_type 2). Doc tests, `RUSTFLAGS=... RUSTDOCFLAGS="--cap-lints warn" TMPDIR=/target/tmp
    cargo test --locked --doc`: 21 passed, 1 ignored. No failures.'
  notes: 'Sandbox /tmp is noexec, so rustdoc doc tests and Miri sysroot builds fail
    with a permission error unless TMPDIR points elsewhere; this is the container,
    not the crate. All native runs use the NEON backend; AVX2 and SSE4.2 were never
    run. x86_64 results come from Miri (--target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, sysroot
    built offline in /work/miri-sysroot-x86), where runtime detection finds no AVX2/SSE4.2
    and takes the native fallback backend. cargo-miri rejects --cap-lints in RUSTFLAGS,
    so Miri reproducers build a copy of the baseline at /target/nolint-base with only
    the `#![deny(warnings)]` line removed. A random token fuzz of public parse, serde,
    tape-walk and encode APIs in debug build (11.6M inputs in total, repro at explore/)
    found no new panics, so crash stays at one entry. Controls: repro/control.sh,
    output in control-output.txt.'
ai_policy: 'none found. Checked README.md, Contribution.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md and
  .github/ (dependabot.yml and workflows); no AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, PR templates or
  docs/ directory. Recent commits carry Claude co-author lines and #471 credits Gemini,
  which is not a policy.'
bugs:
- id: simd-json-5
  kind: wrong-behavior
  summary: Tape Value get_array, get_object, try_get_array and try_get_object slice
    the tape one node short, losing the last element of a nested container.
  location:
  - src/value/tape/trait_impls.rs:425
  - src/value/tape/trait_impls.rs:454
  - src/value/tape/trait_impls.rs:487
  - src/value/tape/trait_impls.rs:519
  confirmed_at_baseline: reproduced
  match_rule: Names the off-by-one slice idx..idx+count in the tape shortcut accessors
    (container header plus count nodes), dropping the last element or leaving an empty
    slice that later panics on empty containers.
  caveat: Kept as wrong-behavior, but the panics on empty containers are a crash from
    valid input on the same defect; a finding that reports only that panic should
    score here. Shared code.
- id: simd-json-6
  kind: wrong-behavior
  summary: Huge exponents wrap through `as i32` and give wrong finite numbers, while
    valid very long exponents are rejected.
  location:
  - src/numberparse/correct.rs:156
  - src/numberparse/correct.rs:186
  confirmed_at_baseline: reproduced
  match_rule: Names narrowing the accumulated i64 exponent with `as i32` (wrapping
    into range and giving a wrong finite value) or the error on exponents past 2^32
    rejecting valid numbers, in the default number parser.
  caveat: Upstream fix is co-authored by Claude. Shared code.
- id: simd-json-7
  kind: wrong-behavior
  summary: Fixed-length serde targets leave unread array elements on the tape, which
    are then read as later values.
  location:
  - src/serde/de.rs:33
  - src/serde/de.rs:34
  - src/serde/de.rs:240
  - src/serde/de.rs:433
  confirmed_at_baseline: reproduced
  match_rule: Names the serde array path handing a visitor CommaSeparated without
    checking afterward that all elements were consumed, so surplus elements shift
    into later fields of a tuple, array or struct target.
  caveat: Upstream fix is co-authored by Claude. Shared code.
- id: simd-json-8
  kind: wrong-behavior
  summary: A lone high surrogate such as "\ud800" parses as NUL (U+0000) instead of
    returning an error.
  location:
  - src/stringparse.rs:61
  - src/stringparse.rs:72
  confirmed_at_baseline: reproduced
  match_rule: Names get_unicode_codepoint returning Ok((0, _)) for a high surrogate
    without a following low surrogate, a leftover failure sentinel that callers now
    take as code point 0, silently decoding it as NUL.
  caveat: RFC 8259 section 8.2 only says lone surrogates give unpredictable behaviour;
    the wrong part is silent NUL where the crate rejects the low-surrogate twin. Upstream
    fix is co-authored by Claude. Parse_str differs per backend but this function
    is shared; run on NEON. `"\ud800\uzzzz"` already errored here on NEON through
    another check.
- id: simd-json-11
  kind: wrong-behavior
  summary: A NUL byte counts as structural-or-whitespace, so a number followed by
    NUL is accepted.
  location:
  - src/charutils.rs:14
  - src/numberparse/correct.rs:180
  - src/numberparse/correct.rs:192
  - src/lib.rs:798
  confirmed_at_baseline: reproduced
  match_rule: Names the STRUCTURAL_OR_WHITESPACE tables treating byte 0 as a valid
    terminator (tied to zero padding of the input buffer), so number parsing accepts
    an embedded NUL after a number.
  caveat: Listed as a possible crash, but it does not panic. The tag has a test that
    asserts this behaviour with a todo comment, so upstream knew. Shared code.
- id: simd-json-12
  kind: wrong-behavior
  summary: Long decimals are corrupted because a byte value is used as an index when
    digit_count is corrected.
  location:
  - src/numberparse/correct.rs:168
  - src/numberparse/correct.rs:172
  confirmed_at_baseline: reproduced
  match_rule: Names `start_digits = get!(buf, start) as usize` in the float path,
    which stores the byte at start rather than the offset, so the leading-zero digit_count
    correction is wrong and long decimals get a wrong value.
  caveat: Listed as a possible crash, but it gives a wrong value and did not panic
    in any run. Shared code.
